# =============================================================================
# DEEP Portfolio - API environment
# =============================================================================
# Copy to .env and fill in real values. Never commit .env.
#
# Every variable below is read somewhere in the code. Values shown are safe
# local-development placeholders, NOT production values.
#
# src/config/env.ts is the only module that reads process.env. It resolves and
# validates everything once at startup, so a missing required value crashes the
# server immediately with a list of what is missing, rather than failing later
# on whichever request touches it first. Variables marked OPTIONAL below have a
# default or may be left blank; everything else is required.
#
# This file is for running the API straight on the host — `npm run dev:api`. It
# is NOT what docker compose reads; that is the .env at the repo root, built
# from .env.example there. Keeping them separate is not duplication: the same
# setting needs different values. Postgres is `db:5432` inside the compose
# network and `localhost:5432` from the host, and the values below are the
# host's.
# =============================================================================

# --- Server ------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — defaults: development, 4001
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=4001

# OPTIONAL — default http://localhost:3000. Origin allowed by CORS, and the base
# of the invite links sent by email. Must match where the web app is served from.
CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Prisma reads DATABASE_URL, and it is now the only database setting there is.
# The discrete DB_HOST / DB_PORT / DB_NAME / DB_USER / DB_PASSWORD variables are
# gone: the only module that read them was src/config/db.ts, which nothing
# imported and which has been deleted (see spec D8).
#
# The password sits inside a URL, so it cannot contain raw URL syntax. A `/` in
# particular ends the authority part early and Prisma reports
#   P1013: The provided database string is invalid. invalid port number
# without mentioning the password at all. Percent-encode : / @ ? # % — or,
# easier, generate a password that has none of them: openssl rand -hex 32
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/deep_portfolio?schema=public

# --- Auth --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48
# There is deliberately no fallback in code: a missing secret must crash
# the server, not silently accept forgeable tokens.
JWT_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=

# The Google OAuth client id both this API and the web app use. Required: the
# API refuses any ID token that was not issued for this exact client, which is
# what stops a valid Google token minted for some other site being replayed
# here as a login.
#
# Not a secret — a client id is public by design and is in the web bundle too.
# There is no client secret in this flow at all: the browser gets the ID token
# straight from Google and this server only verifies it (see spec D3).
#
# Create one at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials as an OAuth
# client of type "Web application", with the web app's origin listed under
# Authorised JavaScript origins (http://localhost:3000 for local work).
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# OPTIONAL — default blank. Domain attribute of the session cookies, used by
# everything that sets or clears them (src/config/cookies.ts). Blank makes them
# host-only: sent back only to the host that set them, which is what a local
# server wants. Set it only when the API and the web app are on different
# subdomains of one domain and the cookie has to cross between them.
COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# --- Object storage (MinIO) --------------------------------------------------
# Credentials the API uses to talk to MinIO. Locally these are the MinIO root
# credentials, i.e. MINIO_ROOT_USER / MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD from the repo-root
# .env — those two configure the MinIO server itself and belong to
# docker-compose.yml, not here; src/config/env.ts does not read them.
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=

MINIO_ENDPOINT=localhost

# OPTIONAL — defaults: 9000, deep-portfolio.
# 9002, not 9000: docker-compose.yml publishes MinIO on host port 9002 and keeps
# 9000 free, because 9000 is also this variable's own default and having both
# mean different things on the same host is a trap. If MinIO is being run some
# other way, set this to whatever port it actually listens on.
MINIO_PORT=9002
MINIO_BUCKET=deep-portfolio

# Presigned URLs come back pointing at the in-network host, which a browser
# cannot resolve. The API rewrites INTERNAL -> PUBLIC before returning them.
MINIO_INTERNAL_HOST=minio:9000
MINIO_PUBLIC_HOST=localhost:9002

# --- Email -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gmail account used to send group-invite emails. This IS live: creating or
# updating a student activity group sends an invite to every member, via
# src/config/mailer.ts. (An earlier note here claimed nodemailer was dead code
# — that was wrong, see spec D8.)
#
# OPTIONAL. Leaving these blank does not break group creation: the send is
# wrapped in try/catch and a failure is logged, not propagated. The group is
# still created, the invite email just never arrives. They are deliberately not
# required, so a blank mail account must not stop the server from starting.
EMAIL_USER=
EMAIL_PASS=
